---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";

---

<Layout
    title="Conclusion & Credits - Replenish Guide"
    description="Reflections on the enduring fellowship of Premodern Magic and honorable acknowledgments celebrating the scholars and pilots who shaped this comprehensive Replenish guide."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Conclusion & Credits"
        description="Reflections on the true spirit of table fellowship in Premodern Magic, accompanied by honorable acknowledgments celebrating the community theorists and pilots who elevated the archetype."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/abeyance.webp"
        imageAlt="Abeyance"
        imageCaption="Abeyance by Thomas Gianni"
        isCard={true}
    />



    <Card title="The Spirit of the Gathering" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;"
        >
            At the end of the day, all the stack mechanics, matchup percentages,
            and sideboard guides serve one simple purpose: <strong
                style="color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                >playing a game we love with people we respect.</strong
            >
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
            Premodern is special precisely because the human connection comes
            first. When we sit down across the table—whether in a regional
            tournament or at a kitchen table—the ultimate victory isn't locking
            an opponent out of the game or lifting a trophy; it's the shared
            laughter, the appreciation for classic old-frame cards, and the
            conversations between rounds. Play hard, play with precision, but
            always remember that the greatest magic is the gathering itself.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Acknowledgments & Community Credits" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;"
        >
            This exhaustive strategic compendium represents the culmination of
            years of rigorous tournament playtesting, mathematical analysis, and
            passionate theoretical refinement within the Premodern competitive
            ecosystem. Deepest gratitude and formal attribution belong to the
            following community builders:
        </p>
        <ul
            style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.75rem;"
        >
            <li>
                <strong>Brian Siu (Dr Siuss):</strong> Author of the original foundational
                Premodern Replenish primer that inspired the educational framework
                and design architecture of this comprehensive, interactive guide.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Gary Roth:</strong> Mathematical theorist and rules scholar
                behind the definitive treatises documenting the formal stack-physics,
                timestamp layering, and priority loops of Opalescent Parallax Wave
                collisions.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The Premodern Replenish Discord Community:</strong> The collective
                brotherhood of passionate tournament pilots, deckbuilders, and researchers
                whose daily rigorous discussions, mulligan evaluations, and collaborative
                passion continue to elevate the archetype. Special thanks to
                <strong>The Atog Lord</strong>, <strong>justint</strong>, <strong
                    >Pylse</strong
                >,
                <strong>Rich_Card_O</strong>, <strong>Eichhorn (Ben V)</strong>,
                <strong>aardshark (galen)</strong>, <strong
                    >Marine Biomancer</strong
                >,
                <strong>Bitner</strong>, <strong>chowder</strong>, <strong
                    >Lannynyny</strong
                >, and <strong>Coinman</strong> for their invaluable insights into
                post-ban opening hand evaluation.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
